WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:17.590
The Wire is a special show.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:22.440
Few television shows inspire a stack of books
about it’s creation and themes, even fewer

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.610
inspire a collection of college courses examining
those same things, and only one has inspired

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.710
sitting the president of the united states
to invite the show’s creator to the White

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.000
House for a conversation.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.250
What is it that makes this show so special?

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:42.110
In this video, part one of my series about
The Wire, I’ll be exploring why The Wire

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.651
is brilliant, without spoiling the plot, so
even if you haven’t seen the show yet, you

00:00:46.651 --> 00:00:48.840
can learn why it’s so special.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:01:13.990
Not A Cop Show
The

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.450
Wire is not a cop show.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:20.940
Yes, a series of police wiretap investigations
is the spine around which the show is constructed,

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.750
but The Wire starts out, at the very least,
as a subversion of the standard cop show,

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:26.750
and only become more from there.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:31.520
Where in most police dramas the moment of
chasing and arresting the suspect is the dramatic

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:38.380
climax, these are often the very moments The
Wire skips over.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:46.370
But The Wire is much more than a subversion
of the classic police drama.

00:01:46.370 --> 00:02:02.220
Season One establishes a failing, corrupt
institution, the Baltimore

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.440
Police Department, and a band of rebels within
its ranks that are fighting desperately to

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:15.870
eek out any semblance of “good police work.”

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:32.440
But what our detective are up against, is
not a clear cut set of “bad guys” but

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:38.770
a rival institution, a Baltimore Drug Gang,
made up of real humans.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:45.450
An institution complete with its own set of
rebels, hoping to swim against the system.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:51.450
Each of the following seasons adds another
layer of the city, examining the institutions

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:57.140
above and below the two featured in season
one.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:02.590
Season Two adds in the decaying port economy
and labor unions, season 3 sees the addition

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.680
of political realm, season 4 adds the school
system, and season 5 journalism.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:19.830
If all this sounds a bit dry and academic,
it certainly is when placed beside your average

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:24.069
episode of Hawaii Five-0.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:37.069
The Wire doesn’t try to

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:38.700
grab and keep your attention.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.000
It requires it, and if you give it your attention
it will reward you.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.819
But it’s also entertaining, the show tells
this story of the city and it’s institutions,

00:03:48.819 --> 00:03:54.430
not through dry exposition about the institutions
but through the individual stories of an incredible

00:03:54.430 --> 00:04:07.310
cast of engaging characters within those institutions,
fantastically acted, and brilliantly written

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:12.020
by a team dedicated to realistically portraying
the city of Baltimore.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.079
All The Pieces Matter: Good Writing

00:04:15.079 --> 00:04:17.759
The Wire has a great acting, cinematography
and sound design, but what stands above everything

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:20.650
else, the key element, is the writing.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:25.280
The Wire reaches a bar for quality of writing
reached by few television shows.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:31.350
Write what you know, is a basic directive
commonly issued to writers, and few TV writers

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:37.320
have exemplified this idea as well as show
creators David Simon and Ed Burns.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.140
David Simon was a reporter for the Baltimore
Sun for 12 years.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:46.040
He covered crime and the police department
extensively, and eventually went on to write

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.389
Homicide: A Year on the Killing Streets and
The Corner: A Year in the Life of an Inner-City

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:54.130
Neighborhood both of which Simon adapted for
television.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:59.030
Collectively all this can be seen as nearly
two decades of writing experience closely

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.152
focused on the topics that would largely be
covered in The Wire.

00:05:02.152 --> 00:05:03.152
David Simon spent years examining the worlds
The Wire explored before one episode was even

00:05:03.152 --> 00:05:04.152
written.

00:05:04.152 --> 00:05:08.080
While he was still a journalist Simon developed
a relationship with Ed Burns, who spent 20

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.210
years working as a Baltimore City Police,
before joining forces with David Simon to

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:14.210
write The Corner.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.699
If Simon had spent 20 years reporting and
writing about the topics that The Wire examines,

00:05:18.699 --> 00:05:21.920
Ed Burns spent 20 years living them.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.291
Including spending time as a public school
teacher in Baltimore, direct experience, on

00:05:27.291 --> 00:05:29.960
which much of Season 4’s story is based.

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:35.190
As Police Detective and Crime Journalist turned
Television Writers, these two were interested

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:39.511
in far more than making another cop show to
entertain the masses.

00:05:39.511 --> 00:05:44.170
They aspired to put a contemporary american
crime novel on the screen.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:49.551
And the writers they brought on to help them
do this came, not from the world of television,

00:05:49.551 --> 00:05:51.510
but from the literary scene.

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:56.320
Novelist Denis Lenhane, George Pelacanos,
and Richard Price would all become critical

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.160
parts of the writing teams.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.790
Authors who didn’t have much experience
writing for TV, but who were all well versed

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:04.500
in Urban Crime-Fiction.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.600
And when Simon and Burns expanded the show
to subjects they didn’t feel they knew as

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:11.199
well, they brought in people who did.

00:06:11.199 --> 00:06:16.050
Rafael Alvarez, another Baltimore journalist,
joined the writing team bringing his knowledge

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.860
of the Baltimore docks for season 2.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:23.320
William F. Zorzi, a Political journalist in
Baltimore, joined to help write a lot of the

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.110
political story that starts in season 3.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:31.720
With their experience as authors and journalists,
these writers brought to the show a greater

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:37.110
expectation for the audience.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:45.930
They required a level of attention from the
viewers of the wire, not normally demanded

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:56.699
by television shows, but a level a step closer
to the level of attention you have to apply

00:06:56.699 --> 00:06:57.699
to reading a book.

00:06:57.699 --> 00:06:58.699
[s1e5 25:30]

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:02.000
This expectation of attention allowed the
writers to include many blink-and-you’ll

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.000
miss it details.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.000
Blink and You’ll Miss It.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.270
Here’s an example [s2e3 13:50] of the type
of quick and subtle storytelling you’ll

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.430
encounter in The Wire.

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:12.370
This scene from Season 2 shows Omar doing
reconnaissance for a stickup location, while

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:18.430
he is, he notices that two ladies are doing
the same thing.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:26.889
And attentive viewer will see what’s happening
in the scene, and understand Omar’s recognition

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:28.139
of what’s going on.

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:35.009
But one glance at your phone, and you might
miss the entire 25 second, dialogue free scene.

00:07:35.009 --> 00:07:37.253
This level of subtly is used frequently throughout
the show.

00:07:37.253 --> 00:07:39.920
Like this scene from the second episode of
season 1, [s1e2 54:30] which begins to setup

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.870
Bodie as a character who is more on top of
things than his colleagues when he walks up

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:48.500
on a bunch of other drug dealers sleeping
on the job.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:08:06.400
Very quick scenes, often won’t payoff until
episodes later.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.229
One of the things that makes The Wire’s
storytelling so powerful, is that while characters

00:08:11.229 --> 00:08:20.889
sometimes do express ideas directly, the most
important and central themes of the show are

00:08:20.889 --> 00:08:26.860
all developed through this type of “show
don’t tell”, storytelling.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:32.710
Season 1 is largely about establishing the
institutional similarities between the drug

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:37.690
dealers and the police officers, but no character
ever comes out and talks about this on the

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:38.880
show.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:44.089
Instead the show relates the two by slowly
building up a collection of parallel scenes.

00:08:44.089 --> 00:08:55.610
Like this scene that shows the police willing
to bend the rules [s1e9 38:14] while a few

00:08:55.610 --> 00:09:05.300
scenes later we get an example of gang members
adhering closely to their rules [s1e9 53:50].

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:09.260
These two simple scenes progress the plot,
but more importantly they’re juxtaposition

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:14.839
shows us how both institutions have rules
for themselves, but that the motivations around

00:09:14.839 --> 00:09:24.330
following those rules lead to different behavior
within the institutions.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:28.570
These types of parallels are also used to
show the similarities between individuals.

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:40.680
Gang members need a judge to mediate their
differences, just like detective McNulty and

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:44.059
his wife.

00:09:44.059 --> 00:09:52.290
Over time this collection of mirrored scenes
accumulates to showcase the similarities in

00:09:52.290 --> 00:10:09.680
these different institutions, and how individuals
behave differently within them.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:14.920
It creates a powerful argument, one that appears
to the viewer as self-evident through the

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:17.079
experience of watching the show.

00:10:17.079 --> 00:10:20.920
But it wasn’t just the writing team’s
experience as writers that made the show what

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:26.569
it is, they utilized the team’s experience
as actual teachers, journalists, police officers

00:10:26.569 --> 00:10:31.309
to create one of the most realistic fictional
shows that’s ever been put on TV.

00:10:31.309 --> 00:10:36.269
A Man Must Have A Code: The Commitment to
Realism

00:10:36.269 --> 00:10:41.679
David Simon and Ed Burns had an extreme level
of commitment to realistically portraying

00:10:41.679 --> 00:10:44.041
Baltimore and the institutions within the
city.

00:10:44.041 --> 00:10:45.041
Let’s look at the steps they took to make
sure they portrayed the character of the city

00:10:45.041 --> 00:10:46.041
as accurately and realistically as possible.

00:10:46.041 --> 00:10:47.041
Their backgrounds as a journalist and investigator
played a role in their approach to writing.

00:10:47.041 --> 00:10:48.209
When they came up against a subject that they
didn’t know inside and out, they conducted

00:10:48.209 --> 00:10:49.610
extensive research.

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:54.129
Whether you’re talking about the interviews
with dozens of stevedores, immigration officials,

00:10:54.129 --> 00:10:58.850
port personnel, customs inspectors and steamship
agents that were conducted before writing

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.540
about the ports in season 2.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:05.889
Or the extensive interviews and ongoing relationships
with Melvin Williams, real life Baltimore

00:11:05.889 --> 00:11:09.579
drug king, who reformed after a long stint
in prison.

00:11:09.579 --> 00:11:14.980
They clearly weren’t willing to write about
topics they hadn’t experience first hand

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:16.720
without extensive research.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:22.009
And the commitment to realism extended beyond
the writing staff to the cast who often went

00:11:22.009 --> 00:11:27.209
to great lengths to accurately portray the
lives their characters represented.

00:11:27.209 --> 00:11:31.850
Andre Royo, who played Bubbles, the show’s
beloved heroin junkie said: “We were inspired

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:36.679
by the city of Baltimore, by the people in
Baltimore, of how these characters were supposed

00:11:36.679 --> 00:11:38.449
to live and be portrayed.”

00:11:38.449 --> 00:11:43.350
His portrayal of a junkie was so refined,
he was once chased of set by the show’s

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:47.970
security, who thought he was a real addict,
and was once handed drugs on the street by

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:51.680
another addict, who mistook Royo for the real
deal.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:57.410
While Simon and Burns both insist that no
character is a direct correlation to a person

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:02.399
in real life, almost no character exists on
the wire that doesn’t have elements of one

00:12:02.399 --> 00:12:04.380
or more real life people.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:09.379
Bubbles was inspired in part by a long standing
informant of Ed Burns, Avon Barksdale and

00:12:09.379 --> 00:12:14.639
Stringer Bell were both informed by actual
drug dealers like Mevlin Williams.

00:12:14.639 --> 00:12:18.930
Williams would actually go on to take a role
in The Wire as the Deacon.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:23.429
He was just one among many Baltimore locals
who had roles in the show.

00:12:23.429 --> 00:12:26.549
Notably Prop Joe, was played by, a Baltimore
native.

00:12:26.549 --> 00:12:36.579
Felicia Pearson, who plays Snoop, came from
the life she ended up portraying on screen.

00:12:36.579 --> 00:12:40.769
Simon and Burns often named characters after
real life inspiration.

00:12:40.769 --> 00:12:45.779
The actual Jay Landsman, who this character
is named after, acts on the show, but despite

00:12:45.779 --> 00:12:50.459
auditioning for the part he’s named after,
he didn’t land the role, and ended up playing

00:12:50.459 --> 00:12:51.459
Lt. Mello.

00:12:51.459 --> 00:12:56.920
Ed Norris, who plays a detective in the show
by the same name, was the actual Police Commissioner

00:12:56.920 --> 00:13:00.500
of Baltimore for 2 years right before the
show aired.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:06.240
Bunk Moreland, Tommy Carcetti, and many others
draw inspiration from real life counterparts,

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:12.009
surprisingly even Omar, perhaps the most stylized
regular character on the show, is a composite

00:13:12.009 --> 00:13:16.670
of six different gun-slingers who made their
living sticking up drug dealers in Baltimore.

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:17.670
Not to mention the locals who filled out the
roles as extras.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:23.119
The inclusion of those who lived and new they
life they portrayed wasn’t just a cosmetic

00:13:23.119 --> 00:13:28.199
choice, they helped keep the show from straying
into the realm of fantasy.

00:13:28.199 --> 00:13:33.089
Steve Earle, who plays Bubble’s Narcotic’s
Anonymous sponsor, and had real life experience

00:13:33.089 --> 00:13:38.790
in NA, said that they were encouraged as a
cast, to speak up if something didn’t feel

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:39.790
right.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:44.899
When a city plays such a vital role in the
story, you have to be sure that you’re accurately

00:13:44.899 --> 00:13:45.940
portraying that place.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:50.269
And when you watch The Wire, you can be sure
that what you’re seeing is actually Baltimore.

00:13:50.269 --> 00:13:53.240
The show shot extensively on location.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:57.290
Row houses like these, were often cleaned
out, and re-dressed to look the same, but

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:02.290
with fake dog poop, needles, and broken drug
vials instead of the real thing.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:07.029
Those from Baltimore who worked on the show
were often struck by how real, the portrayal

00:14:07.029 --> 00:14:08.339
of the city was.

00:14:08.339 --> 00:14:13.780
Laura Schweigman a Script Supervisor on the
show said about the opening scene: “I see

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:16.290
this when I drive around in Baltimore.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:22.619
It was sad, but it was, at the same timer,
amazing, how real it felt, how real it looked.”

00:14:22.619 --> 00:14:27.029
Fredo Star, who played Bird a drug dealer
in season one relates: “This is very real

00:14:27.029 --> 00:14:28.029
out here…

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:32.040
… When I got thrown on the floor in one
of the scenes… ...glass was by my face.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.509
It was like, “Hold up, let’s shoot the
glass.”

00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:35.600
It was real.”

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:36.600
And it wasn’t just about making Baltimore
look like Baltimore.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:37.600
West Baltimore looks like West Baltimore,
and East like East.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:38.600
In a scene like this one, where street names
come into play.

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:45.199
The names and locations used are accurate,
to the extent that you can actually map out

00:14:45.199 --> 00:14:46.379
the chase.

00:14:46.379 --> 00:14:50.189
Frequent episode director and producer Joe
Chappelle recalls: “...when they would write

00:14:50.189 --> 00:14:55.350
something and it was on a corner, whatever
the street corner was, we would shoot on that

00:14:55.350 --> 00:14:56.350
corner.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:57.350
It wasn’t like we go to a fun location and
put up fake signs.

00:14:57.350 --> 00:14:58.350
We’d go to that specific corner.”

00:14:58.350 --> 00:14:59.350
The Production
The commitment to realism extend into the

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:00.350
show’s style itself.

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:03.399
The Wire never utilizes a score, except in
a few select cases, all music in the show

00:15:03.399 --> 00:15:06.359
comes from a source within the world.

00:15:06.359 --> 00:15:11.869
But any hole left by score is filled by expertly
used sound design which helps to reinforce

00:15:11.869 --> 00:15:19.579
the emotion of the story, and allows the city
to be a non-stop presence audibly in the show.

00:15:19.579 --> 00:15:21.480
Use of diegetic music in the show is often
brilliant.

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:25.499
Like this chase scene, where the music in
playing Hurc’s car, and how it cuts in and

00:15:25.499 --> 00:15:43.720
out, is used for comedic effect.

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:47.670
The shows cinematography exemplifies the same
ethos.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:53.420
Committed heavily to a naturalistic approach,
without resorting to a documentary stylization,

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:59.259
the visuals manage to be striking and beautiful,
but always serving the locations and characters,

00:15:59.259 --> 00:16:10.000
and rarely calling attention to itself.

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:23.189
Conclusion
The Wire’s story is undeniably political.

00:16:23.189 --> 00:16:28.079
But the way it goes about telling that story,
through its commitment to realism, and by

00:16:28.079 --> 00:16:33.350
painting a portrait of it’s themes and of
the institutions through a collection of individual

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.859
stories, keeps it from being propagandistic.

00:16:36.859 --> 00:16:54.650
The show rarely prescribes a solution, and
seeks mostly to illustrate the problems, very

00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:55.939
real ones.

00:16:55.939 --> 00:17:02.519
Problems that continues unresolved to this
day.

00:17:02.519 --> 00:17:04.089
The Wire is not a cop show.

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:07.260
There’s isn’t a simple good guys and bad
guys dichotomy.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:12.290
We’re introduced to bad police doing the
right thing for the wrong reasons, and gang

00:17:12.290 --> 00:17:18.069
members who seem to have just as much heart
as those on the side of the law.

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:22.829
Evil is not easily rooted out, we can barely
tell who isn’t tainted by malevolence.

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:29.160
The audience cannot root for order to be restored,
because order itself is corrupt.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:34.040
Those who are willing to buck against the
systems do so out of desperation, because

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:37.120
they see no future for themselves in those
systems.

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:42.070
In Part II I’ll explore these themes in
greater depth, and how the wire uses narrative

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:45.900
structure and other techniques to portray
them.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:18:41.700
So if you enjoyed this video, make sure you’re
subscribed, and keep your eyes open for

00:18:41.700 --> 00:18:42.880
part II.

